<article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">medscimonit-17-7-cq3</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Clinical Quiz</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Rare complication after mitral valve replacement due to infective endocarditis (Part 2)</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Abramczuk</surname><given-names>El&#x0017c;bieta</given-names></name><xref rid="af1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Heretyk-Grabowska</surname><given-names>Hanna</given-names></name><xref rid="af1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Grabowski</surname><given-names>Maciej</given-names></name><xref rid="af1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Demkow</surname><given-names>Marcin</given-names></name><xref rid="af2-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="aff">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Witkowski</surname><given-names>Adam</given-names></name><xref rid="af3-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="aff">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Konka</surname><given-names>Marek</given-names></name><xref rid="af4-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="aff">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hryniewiecki</surname><given-names>Tomasz</given-names></name><xref rid="af1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="aff">1</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="af1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3">
<label>1</label>Department of Valvular Heart Disease, Institute of Cardiology, Warsaw, Poland</aff><aff id="af2-medscimonit-17-7-cq3">
<label>2</label>Department of Coronary and Structural Heart Diseases, Institute of Cardiology, Warsaw, Poland</aff><aff id="af3-medscimonit-17-7-cq3">
<label>3</label>Department of Cardiology and Interventional Angiology, Institute of Cardiology, Warsaw, Poland</aff><aff id="af4-medscimonit-17-7-cq3">
<label>4</label>Department of Congenital Heart Disease, Institute of Cardiology, Warsaw, Poland</aff><author-notes><corresp id="c1-medscimonit-17-7-CQ3">Author&#x02019;s Addres: Maciej Grabowski, Department of Valvular Heart Disease, Institute of Cardiology, Alpejska 42 Str., 04-628 Warsaw, Poland, e-mail: <email>grabi71@yahoo.com</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><year>2011</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>01</day><month>7</month><year>2011</year></pub-date><volume>17</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>CQ3</fpage><lpage>CQ6</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; Med Sci Monit, 2011</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2011</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This work is licensed under Creative Common Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">CC BY-NC-ND 4.0</ext-link>)</license-p></license></permissions><abstract><p>Summary</p><p>A 27-year-old woman who had undergone mitral valve replacement for infective endocarditis developed a significant paravalvular leak. Percutaneous transcatheter obliteration of the defect using an Amplatzer Vascular Plug III was undertaken, with an excellent clinical outcome.</p></abstract><kwd-group><title>key words</title><kwd>paravalvular leak</kwd><kwd>mitral valve replacement</kwd><kwd>percutaneous closure of the leak</kwd><kwd>infectious endocrditis</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="other"><title>Part II</title><sec><title>Answers to the Questions</title><p>Our case, a 27-year-old female patient who had undergone mitral valve replacement for infective endocarditis, developed a significant paravalvular leak. Paravalvular leak is a rare complication after mitral valve replacement (MVR), occurring in 1% to 5% of patients [<xref rid="b1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>]. PVL may be associated with the suturing technique used, or with previous surgery, but more often PVL appears in patients with a disease of the mitral annulus as a result of active infective endocarditis. Small jets usually do not cause severe complications. A large regurgitant flow can lead to severe hemolytic anemia or cause progressive left ventricular dilatation and congestive heart failure. In the case presented, examinations initially performed did not demonstrate a clinically significant leak, and the patient was discharged home, but echocardiographic examinations performed in the 1<sup>st</sup>, 2<sup>nd</sup> and 6<sup>th</sup> months after surgery revealed an increasing paravalvular leak.</p><p>To confirm a clinically significant PVL, a detailed medical history for symptoms of heart failure should be collected, and echocardiography (TTE and TEE) should be performed. The patient reported a small reduction of physical capacity, with slight exertional dyspnea. Echocardiography revealed the diameter of the left ventricle (LVDD &#x02013; 41 mm) and systolic function (EF &#x02013; 70%) were normal, but the left atrium increased in diameter from 33 mm to 45 mm.</p><sec><title>QUESTION</title><p>2: <italic>What blood tests should be done to assess hemolysis severity?</italic></p><p>Blood tests revealed a significantly increased level of lactate dehydrogenase (500 U/L), an increased level of bilirubin (40.8 &#x003bc;mol/l), RBC 3.26 m/&#x003bc;l, hemoglobin 10.0 g/dl, and a significantly increased level of NT pro-BNP (3174 pg/ml). These results indicated severe hemolysis and heart failure.</p><p>In some cases the causative factor for PVL is IE, which leads to paravalvular abscess formation, its evacuation and leak. Early IE was considered as a factor for PVL in our patient. Fortunately, TEE examination revealed no bacterial vegetations on the mitral valve prosthesis and no signs of a paravalvular abscess. In addition, blood cultures taken during the post-operative period were negative. Hence, the causative factor was probably an organically modified annulus, whose connective tissue had been weakened and dissolved in the post-IE process, which caused the sutures to rip out.</p></sec><sec><title>QUESTION</title><p>3: <italic>What are the treatment options?</italic></p><p>Considering the appearance of heart failure symptoms, increasing diameter of the left atrium, and blood tests findings, the PVL was estimated as hemodynamically and clinically significant. A decision to re-do the procedure was made. The patient was informed of the treatment options: to re-do mitral surgery or percutaneous closure. Surgery is considered the gold standard of dehiscence repair, but reoperation is associated with significant morbidity and mortality rates. The mortality rate in valve reoperations is about 10% [<xref rid="b2-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>]. The operating options are to sew an artificial valve ring onto the mitral annulus or to re-replace the valve. In recent years, the use of percutaneous closure devices for closing PVLs has been proposed as an alternative to surgery. This technique was first reported in 1992 by Hourihan et al, who closed PVLs using Rashkind devices [<xref rid="b3-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]; this method is still not routinely performed. Published data show a technical success rate of 60% to 90% and a need for repeat intervention in up to 40% of cases [<xref rid="b4-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>]. The long-term clinical outcome of percutaneous closure of PVLs is unknown. Another aspect of the procedure is lack of specific devices designed for such procedures. All devices used in PVLs closure are considered to be off-label, thus, the procedure should be proposed only to patients with a very high or unacceptable risk for reoperation, or to those with clinical consequences, who do not agree for re-do surgery. Patients should be clearly informed of the risks and benefits of off-label device use and should give their informed consent for the procedure.</p><p>The patient agreed to undergo percutaneous closure of the paravalvular leak, and the procedure was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Institute of Cardiology.</p><p>She was placed under general anesthesia and a TEE probe was positioned at the beginning of the procedure and remained there throughout its course. For precise visualization of the leakage, surrounding structures and wire navigation, 3-dimensional real-time TEE (RT3D TEE) was used. Mitral PVL closure was performed using the anterograde approach. For this purpose a femoro-femoral wire loop was constructed to deliver the closure device. Sheaths were placed in the right femoral artery and vein. Transseptal puncture was performed using a Brockenbrough needle and a transseptal 8.5F St. Jude sheath was advanced into the left atrium. A steerable electrode (7F) was inserted into this sheath and used to cross the leak into the left ventricle under RT3D TEE control. Using an arterial approach, an Amplatz left catheter (AL.2, 6F) was advanced into the left ventricle and a wire was inserted through this catheter. The steerable electrode was captured by the wire and the Amplatz catheter was drawn through the leak into the left atrium and then into the transseptal sheath. Using this system, the transseptal sheath was advanced through the leak into the left ventricle (<xref rid="f1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="fig">Figure 1</xref>). The wire was released, and the steerable electrode and Amplatz catheter were removed. Through the transseptal sheath, an Amplatzer Vascular Plug III (waist size 5 mm, disk diameter 14 mm) was advanced into the left ventricle. Under RT3D TEE guidance, the device was positioned into the leak. The distal disk was opened and positioned on the ventricle part of the leak and leaned on the edge of the orifice (<xref rid="f2-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="fig">Figure 2</xref>). Then the device was pulled and the proximal disk was opened in the atrial chamber. After echocardiography confirmed a successful closure, the device was detached (<xref rid="f3-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="fig">Figures 3</xref><xref rid="f4-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="fig"/>&#x02013;<xref rid="f5-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="fig">5</xref>). Left atrial pressure decreased from 12/18/12 mmHg to 5/8/4 mmHg.</p><p>The patient tolerated the procedure well and was discharged from hospital on the 2<sup>nd</sup> post-procedure day. At 4-month follow-up, she was in good condition and presented no residual leak in echocardiography.</p><p>In this case a percutaneous closure of the PVL was an attractive method for the young patient. Experience has shown that the best results may be achieved in patients with a small to medium sized (&#x0003c;5 mm) paravalvular leak [<xref rid="b5-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>], as was the case in our patient. However, it must be emphasized that very little data exists on the percutaneous closure of PVLs, and there are no devices specifically designed for such procedures. Existing devices are approved for the closure of other cardiovascular defects (e.g., ASD, VSD, PDA) [<xref rid="b4-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>], and using them for PVL closure is considered off-label use. The patient must be informed in detail of the potential risks and benefits of off-label device use. A very careful examination of the leak&#x02019;s anatomy and the related structures is a critical point of the procedure. The length and diameter of the defect determine the waist and diameter of the device used. The Amplatzer family of devices is most frequently used for PVL closure. It includes 5 devices: Amplatzer Septal Occluder, Amplatzer Muscular VSD Occluder, Amplatzer Duct Occluder, Amplatzer Vascular Plug, and Vascular Coils. RT3D TEE reconstruction of the anatomic view of the leak and surrounding structures allowed the best occluder to be chosen. The close proximity of the PVL to the prosthetic valve makes it possible for the device&#x02019;s disk to interact with valve leaflets. Echocardiography should specify the location of the defect and the distance from the surrounding intracardiac structures, and this should help in selecting the correctly sized device. Another reason for using an Amplatzer device is that it can be retrieved completely into the sheath when the positioning is unsatisfactory in terms of residual leak, unstable positioning, or interference with the prosthetic valve [<xref rid="b6-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>].</p><p>Two-dimensional transesophageal echocardiography (2-D TEE) is not ideal for visualizing an intracardiac catheter [<xref rid="b7-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>], so real-time 3-dimensional transesophageal echocardiography (RT3-D TEE) was used to help guide this catheter-based procedure. Simultaneous fluoroscopy and RT3D TEE allowed precise maneuvering and positioning of the steerable electrode through the leak. By using a steerable electrode with variable curvature as a guidewire, we could maneuver its tip precisely, as during ablation procedures, and move throughout the dehiscence. RT3D TEE confirmed that the steerable electrode had passed through the target orifice, and was able to create color full-volume reconstructions visualizing the paravalvular leak [<xref rid="b8-medscimonit-17-7-cq3" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>]. Post-PVL closure RT3D TEE examination proved the device was stabile and that there was no residual paravalvular leak.</p></sec></sec></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions"><title>Conclusions</title><p>The percutaneous closure of PVL with an Amplatzer occluder is feasible, but long-term follow-up is required to assure the safety and effectiveness of the procedure.</p><p>There is also a need to engineer a closure device designed specifically for such procedures.</p></sec></body><back><ref-list><title>References</title><ref id="b1-medscimonit-17-7-cq3"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="journal">
